<div class="wrapper">
  <h1 data-i18n="whylc.heading">Why Live in Las Cruces</h1>
  <p class="why-las-cruces__intro" data-i18n="whylc.intro">Thinking about a move to southern New Mexico? Here's what draws people to Las Cruces — and what tends to keep them here.</p>
</div>

<section class="services-grid wrapper">
  <div class="service-card">
    <h3 data-i18n="whylc.card1h">Sunshine Almost Every Day</h3>
    <p data-i18n="whylc.card1b">Las Cruces sees sunshine on the vast majority of days each year, with short, mild winters that rarely bring snow or ice. It's an easy place to spend more time outdoors, and the consistent sun makes solar panels a genuinely good investment here.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="service-card">
    <h3 data-i18n="whylc.card2h">Housing That Doesn't Break the Budget</h3>
    <p data-i18n="whylc.card2b">Home prices and property taxes here tend to run well below the national average, and below New Mexico's bigger cities too. That affordability spans a real range of styles, from newer builds to classic adobe character homes.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="service-card">
    <h3 data-i18n="whylc.card3h">The Organ Mountains Are Always Nearby</h3>
    <p data-i18n="whylc.card3b">The Organ Mountains frame the skyline from almost anywhere in town, and the Organ Mountains-Desert Peaks National Monument puts serious hiking and mountain biking within a short drive. White Sands National Park's gypsum dunes make for an easy day trip.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="service-card">
    <h3 data-i18n="whylc.card4h">A College Town With Room to Grow</h3>
    <p data-i18n="whylc.card4b">New Mexico State University anchors the city with affordable degree programs and a steady stream of campus events, lectures, and sports. It also brings a younger energy and a wider range of things to do than a city this size usually has.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="service-card">
    <h3 data-i18n="whylc.card5h">Chile, Culture, and a Real Food Scene</h3>
    <p data-i18n="whylc.card5b">Local restaurants lean into New Mexican cooking, and the city's closeness to Hatch keeps the chile supply — red, green, or "Christmas" — genuinely fresh. A downtown farmers and crafts market runs twice a week, rain or shine.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="service-card">
    <h3 data-i18n="whylc.card6h">Art, Music, and Something Going On</h3>
    <p data-i18n="whylc.card6b">A cluster of local galleries and a busy calendar of festivals — food, wine, film, and more — give Las Cruces more cultural texture than its size suggests. Live music turns up in restaurants, wineries, and breweries around town most weeks.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="service-card">
    <h3 data-i18n="whylc.card7h">Close to Everything, Congested by Nothing</h3>
    <p data-i18n="whylc.card7b">El Paso's international airport is under an hour away, Albuquerque is a manageable drive north, and Southwest landmarks like Carlsbad Caverns and the Gila Wilderness are all within a few hours. Around town, most errands are a short drive without big-city traffic.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="service-card">
    <h3 data-i18n="whylc.card8h">A Comfortable Place to Settle In</h3>
    <p data-i18n="whylc.card8b">Mild weather, a lower cost of living, and accessible healthcare make Las Cruces a popular choice for retirees and anyone looking to slow down a little. The mix of small-town warmth and real amenities tends to make new arrivals feel at home fast.</p>
  </div>
</section>

